:name: dya2 wired split -- central Studio over USB CDC + wired split link (uart0) to a real peripheral
:description: Two machines. central (dya2_right) = the real studio-rpc-usb-uart image
:description: on the USB+PMW3610 platform, Studio over emulated USB CDC; peripheral
:description: (dya2_left) = the real wired-split half on a Python-stub platform. Both
:description: halves' uart0 (the zmk,wired-split half-duplex link, 19200 baud, P0.03)
:description: are cross-connected through ONE Renode UART hub so the central's
:description: wired-split machinery has a peer and stops starving the USB work-queue.
:description: renode_serve.py preloads each half's NVS, `start`s, then attaches the
:description: DualCdcAcmBridge to the CENTRAL.

# WHY two machines: booted alone, the dya2 central's zmk,wired-split transport
# (uart0, half-duplex, INTERRUPT mode) never sees a peer, and its split
# machinery starves the Zephyr USB device work-queue thread -- SET_ADDRESS is
# ACKed in ISR but GET_DESCRIPTOR(CONFIGURATION)/SET_CONFIGURATION get no device
# response, so the CDC never wires and dya-studio cannot connect. Giving the
# wired link a real peripheral peer (its uart0 on the same hub) lets the split
# handshake complete, freeing the USB WQ so enumeration finishes.
#
# The split link is on uart0 here (NOT uart1 as in zmk-west-commands'
# split_wired.resc / usb_wired_split.resc) because dya2's `zmk,wired-split`
# device is &uart0. Half-duplex = one shared wire: both machines' uart0 connect
# to the SAME hub, so each hears the other (a Renode UART hub forwards a
# transmit to every OTHER connected UART, never back to the sender -- exactly a
# point-to-point wire between the two halves).
#
# uart1 on each half is idle (a real image has no UART Studio transport and
# CONFIG_CONSOLE=n on both halves); it is exposed on a server socket only so
# renode_serve.py can own a terminal per machine (Renode serves each terminal to
# only its first client) for symmetry with the single-machine path.

using sysbus

# $central_platform / $peripheral_platform are the vendored repls with model
# `filename:`/`include` paths rewritten to absolute, materialized by
# renode_serve.py (Renode resolves a PythonPeripheral filename against neither
# the .repl dir nor its cwd).
$central_bin?=@central.elf
$peripheral_bin?=@peripheral.elf
$central_platform?=@platforms/xiao_nrf52840_usb_pmw3610.repl
$peripheral_platform?=@platforms/xiao_nrf52840_dya2_peripheral.repl
$central_diag_port?=3480
$peripheral_diag_port?=3481
# Both halves are xiao_ble//zmk images linked at the Adafruit-UF2 bootloader
# offset (CONFIG_FLASH_LOAD_OFFSET); the reset vector -- and every UART/GPIO IRQ
# vector -- lives there, not at 0x0. LoadELF sets the entry PC, but `start`
# resets the core via VTOR, so point VectorTableOffset at the offset after each
# LoadELF or the split UARTE RX ISR never fires.
$vtor?=0x27000

emulation CreateUARTHub "split_link"

# ---- central (real studio image + PMW3610 trackball; wired split on uart0) ---
mach create "dya2_right"
machine LoadPlatformDescription $central_platform
connector Connect sysbus.uart0 split_link
emulation CreateServerSocketTerminal $central_diag_port "central_diag_term" false
connector Connect sysbus.uart1 central_diag_term
sysbus LoadELF $central_bin
sysbus.cpu VectorTableOffset $vtor

# ---- peripheral (real wired-split half on Python-stub platform) --------------
mach create "dya2_left"
machine LoadPlatformDescription $peripheral_platform
connector Connect sysbus.uart0 split_link
emulation CreateServerSocketTerminal $peripheral_diag_port "peripheral_diag_term" false
connector Connect sysbus.uart1 peripheral_diag_term
sysbus LoadELF $peripheral_bin
sysbus.cpu VectorTableOffset $vtor
